"""Write first-principles notes for D3 batch 054."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent; DATA=HERE/'data'; REPORTS=HERE/'reports'
captured={x['paper_id']:x for x in json.loads((DATA/'interspeech-2025-fiftyfourth-d3-papers.json').read_text())['papers']}; papers={x['paper_id']:x for x in json.loads((DATA/'interspeech-2025-papers.json').read_text())['papers']}
N={
 'elhajal25_interspeech':('voice-generation-and-control','voice-identity-and-conversion','voice-conversion','ASR should recover dysarthric speech even when slow rhythm and unusual voice quality make the acoustic pattern unlike healthy training speech.','Speaker variation and rate changes are entangled, and a conversion that sounds healthier may alter linguistic content or fail for severe dysarthria.','Apply a generic speed perturbation or train a speaker-specific converter and assume the recognizer will adapt.','Use unsupervised rhythm and voice conversion with syllable-level rhythm modeling, then measure whether converted speech helps LF-MMI and Whisper ASR.','RnV converts timing and voice characteristics without parallel healthy speech; syllable structure supplies a dysarthria-relevant rhythm representation before ASR scoring.','Conversion is a task-conditioned invariance: remove cues that obstruct recognition while retaining phonetic content needed by the recognizer.','On Torgo, LF-MMI shows reported WER reductions, especially for severe dysarthria, while Whisper fine-tuning on converted data has minimal effect.','Torgo speakers, severity, conversion fidelity, ASR backend, and WER protocol bound transfer; improved recognition is not improved naturalness or clinical communication.'),
 'lee25e_interspeech':('voice-generation-and-control','voice-identity-and-conversion','speaker-identity','A speaker-similarity system should say not only whether two voices match but which voice attributes support that judgment.','Ordinary embeddings compress identity into opaque coordinates, making errors and shifts across tasks difficult to interpret.','Use a black-box speaker embedding and treat its similarity score as a complete explanation.','Represent identity with an interpretable vector of explicit voice-attribute class probabilities and compare its similarity with conventional embeddings.','Vo-Ve maps speech to attribute probabilities; similarity can then be decomposed into human-readable properties instead of a single latent distance.','The representation trades some compact opacity for an interpretable coordinate system whose attributes can be inspected when similarity changes.','The paper reports competitive speaker-similarity evaluation and attribute-level explanations in its experiments.','Attribute inventory, labels, language, channel variation, and metric protocol bound interpretability; explainable similarity is not proof of causal identity factors.'),
 'choi25g_interspeech':('recognition-and-alignment','boundaries-and-sequence-structure','long-context-decoding','A recognizer should handle both spoken-form and written-form transcripts without inconsistent normalization of numbers, names, or punctuation.','Speech databases often contain one transcription convention while modern models emit another, and manually building paired forms is expensive.','Train on whichever transcript convention is available or attach a one-way text-normalization postprocessor.','Use LLM-generated dual transcriptions, iterative supervised/semi-supervised expansion, and a bidirectional text-conversion model supporting both ITN and TN.','The model learns mappings in both directions; generated paired text supplies supervision while iterative learning enlarges the conversion data.','The central object is a representation boundary between spoken language and written conventions, not a change to the acoustic evidence itself.','The paper reports a 13.4% ERR improvement in the evaluated conversion setting.','LLM generation quality, language conventions, error metric, transcript domain, and iterative-label bias bound transfer; normalization success is not ASR acoustic accuracy.'),
 'kirkland25_interspeech':('recognition-and-alignment','boundaries-and-sequence-structure','disfluency-preservation','Listeners may use pauses and disfluencies as evidence when deciding whom or what to believe, so removing them can change behavior rather than merely readability.','Prior ratings of competence do not show whether speech cues affect consequential choices, and credibility judgments interact with source conflict and incentives.','Normalize all disfluent speech before evaluation or infer real-world behavior from Likert ratings alone.','Use an incentivized web decision task with conflicting information and compare choices as a function of speech fluency.','The experiment treats disfluency as part of the observed communication signal and measures choice behavior rather than only an attitude rating.','Behavioral choice is a downstream proxy: it tests whether a listener’s interpretation of fluency changes action under incentives.','The study reports that listeners take speech fluency into account when deciding whom or what to believe.','Task stakes, speakers, disfluency types, online sample, and source-conflict design bound transfer; choice bias is not proof that disfluencies carry truthful information.'),
 'casanova25_interspeech':('evaluation-and-deployment','robustness-and-system-boundary','latency-and-resource','A speech codec used inside an autoregressive audio model should compress sound with few tokens per second without destroying reconstruction quality.','High frame rates make every generated second expensive, while lowering rate or bitrate can erase fine acoustic detail and causality can add delay.','Choose a low frame rate without measuring the rate/bitrate/causality tradeoff, or optimize only waveform quality at an impractical token rate.','Ablate frame rate, bitrate, and causality, then design NanoCodec around a low-rate operating point and compare reconstruction quality.','The codec maps audio to discrete tokens and back; token rate controls autoregressive steps while bitrate and causal context control information and latency.','Compression is a rate-distortion-resource tradeoff: fewer symbols reduce computation but constrain what the decoder can reconstruct.','NanoCodec reports high-quality compression at 12.5 FPS and competitive results across bitrate ranges.','Audio domain, codec training data, perceptual metric, hardware, and causality setting bound transfer; codec quality is not end-to-end speech generation quality.'),
 'ko25_interspeech':('evaluation-and-deployment','robustness-and-system-boundary','distribution-shift','An ASR model should generalize when test speech differs from training speech instead of memorizing redundant or narrow feature patterns.','CTC, AED, and RNN-T models can overfit in different ways, and increasing feature diversity without controlling redundancy may not help.','Add capacity or regularize all features identically without identifying whether redundancy or lack of diversity causes the error.','Add diversity loss to separate feature representations and independence loss to reduce covariance, then test across three ASR architectures.','The losses operate on learned feature vectors: one discourages collapse toward similar patterns, the other discourages redundant correlated dimensions.','The method shapes representation geometry so multiple informative directions survive while redundant variation is suppressed.','The paper reports improved generalization and robustness for CTC, AED, and RNN-T models in the evaluated tasks.','Training/test shifts, loss weights, architectures, languages, and robustness protocol bound transfer; benchmark generalization is not immunity to arbitrary distribution shift.'),
 'gaznepoglu25_interspeech':('people-variation-and-health','speaker-characteristics','speaker-verification','A voice-privacy attack should not mistake repeated linguistic content for speaker identity when evaluating anonymization.','If attacker training and test utterances share semantic or lexical content, text alone can predict the speaker and make privacy scores misleading.','Use a standard ASV attack and assume its error reflects only acoustic identity leakage.','Adapt BERT to attack speaker identity from transcript content, inspect explainable keywords, and compare the resulting EER and dataset construction.','The attacker maps linguistic content to speaker labels; semantically similar utterances become a non-acoustic identity channel in the evaluation.','Privacy evaluation requires separating nuisance correlations from the protected attribute; otherwise the attack measures corpus curation rather than voice leakage.','The paper reports mean EER around 35%, with some speakers as low as 2%, using text alone on VoicePrivacy data.','Dataset curation, speaker/content overlap, BERT training, split design, and EER interpretation bound the claim; text leakage does not prove an anonymizer fails acoustically.'),
 'harrington25_interspeech':('people-variation-and-health','speaker-characteristics','speaker-verification','A speaker-recognition benchmark should reveal which people and conditions remain difficult, not only report one aggregate score across model generations.','Architectural improvements can hide persistent speaker-level failures, and file-level factors can be confused with stable person-level difficulty.','Compare only aggregate metrics or assume each new generation improves every speaker equally.','Evaluate four generations on the same forensic test/calibration data at both system and individual-speaker levels.','Matched evaluation separates model-generation effects from test-set changes and decomposes variation by file and speaker factors.','The unit of analysis matters: an overall metric averages heterogeneous difficulty, while per-speaker outcomes expose persistent tails.','Performance improves from GMM-UBM through i-vector and x-vector but not ECAPA-TDNN in the reported comparison; some individuals remain difficult across systems.','Forensic data, calibration, system implementations, speaker sampling, and metric choice bound transfer; persistent difficulty is not automatically a biological property.'),
}
notes=[]; claims=[]; md=['# INTERSPEECH 2025 fifty-fourth-pass full-paper notes','','Eight official-PDF readings deepen voice conversion/identity, sequence boundaries, resource and distribution robustness, and speaker-level evaluation.','']
for i,(pid,f) in enumerate(N.items(),1):
 theme,sub,concept,bp,wh,naive,ap,mech,math,ww,limits=f; p=papers[pid]; c=captured[pid]
 note={'paper_id':pid,'title':p['title'],'theme_id':theme,'subtheme_id':sub,'concept_id':concept,'bp':bp,'wh':wh,'naive':naive,'ap':ap,'mech':mech,'math':math,'eval':math,'ww':ww,'limits':limits,'dots':{'theme_id':theme,'subtheme_id':sub,'concept_id':concept,'sibling_family':'batch-054'},'source':{'kind':'official-isca-archive-paper-page-plus-captured-pdf-text','pdf_sha256':c['pdf_sha256'],'full_text_sha256':c['full_text_sha256'],'pdf_path':f'data/interspeech-2025-pdfs/{pid}.pdf','text_path':c['full_text_path']},'depth':'D3','evidence_boundary':'D3 structured analyst note backed by captured PDF/text; result remains author-reported and was not independently reproduced.'}
 notes.append(note); claims.append({'claim_id':f'IS25-D3C54-{i:02d}','paper_id':pid,'subtheme':sub,'claim':ww,'evidence_depth':'D3','pdf_sha256':c['pdf_sha256'],'full_text_sha256':c['full_text_sha256'],'support_status':'paper-reported-not-independently-verified','independent_support_status':'not-established','limitations':limits})
 md += [f'## {i}. {sub}','',f"**Paper:** [{p['title']}]({p['paper_url']})",f"**Evidence:** D3; PDF SHA-256 `{c['pdf_sha256']}`; full text captured.",'']+[f'- **{label}:** {note[key]}' for label,key in zip(['Ordinary problem','Why hard','Naive attempt','Central move','Mechanism','Mathematical/conceptual structure','What paper reports','Limits'],['bp','wh','naive','ap','mech','math','ww','limits'])]+['']
(DATA/'interspeech-2025-fiftyfourth-d3-notes.json').write_text(json.dumps({'venue':'INTERSPEECH 2025','evidence_depth':'D3','notes':notes},indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n'); (DATA/'interspeech-2025-fiftyfourth-claim-ledger.json').write_text(json.dumps({'venue':'INTERSPEECH 2025','claim_count':len(claims),'claims':claims},indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n'); REPORTS.mkdir(exist_ok=True); (REPORTS/'INTERSPEECH_2025_FIFTYFOURTH_D3_NOTES.md').write_text('\n'.join(md)+'\n'); print(json.dumps({'notes':len(notes),'claims':len(claims)}))
